+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-17-harden-json-patch-validation/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+## Context
+
+`validate` in `src/index.ts` destructures `op`, `path` and `from` from the operation, which reads inherited properties, while `value` is checked with `Object.hasOwn`. RFC 6902 §3/§4 describes operations as JSON objects, whose members are always own; JSON has no `undefined`.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Read `op`, `path`, `from` and `value` via an `own()` helper returning `undefined` unless `Object.hasOwn`. This is consistent with the existing own-member rule for pointers (RFC 6901) and hostile-key handling.
+- Treat `value: undefined` as missing: RFC 6902 §4.1/4.3/4.6 require a JSON `value`, and `undefined` would otherwise be stored or compared as a non-JSON value.
+- All validation failures already use `JsonPatchError`; keep that, and tests assert the class.
+
+## Risks
+
+- Breaking only for callers passing non-JSON patches built in JavaScript; flagged in the proposal. No standard-vs-convenience ambiguity remains, so the standard wins.
